---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import PipelineDiagram from '@/components/PipelineDiagram.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'
---

<Doc
  title="Redaction and retention"
  description="The redaction rules mcpd applies before it writes anything, the four modes, why the hash is keyed, and how stored data expires or is erased on request."
  lede="What never gets stored, and what stops being stored. Redaction runs before the write, because a secret deleted afterwards was still written down."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Redaction runs before storage</h2>
  <p>
    Prompts and completions are the most valuable thing mcpd holds and the most
    dangerous. Redaction is stage three of the ingest pipeline, ahead of the
    write, so a value a rule matches never reaches the database at all.
  </p>

  <PipelineDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    The ordering is the whole point. A value scrubbed after the fact has still
    been on disk, in the write-ahead log, and quite possibly in last night's
    backup. Never written is a different guarantee from deleted later, and it
    is the only one you can state plainly to somebody who asks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Redaction is on by default. A tool that only protects you once you have
    configured it protects nobody. <code>--no-redact</code>, or
    <code>MCPD_NO_REDACT</code>, turns it off, which is sensible only when
    something upstream already redacts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Redaction is the daemon's end of the wire. The SDKs hold the other end:
    <code>MCPD_CAPTURE_PAYLOADS=false</code>, or <code>capture_payloads</code>
    in code, keeps prompts and completions in the process. A span still
    arrives with its timing, status, usage, model, correlation and errors, and
    <code>input</code> and <code>output</code> are dropped before they are
    queued, so they never cross the network. A team that must not send the
    bytes at all sets that, and a span that holds nothing sensitive can opt
    back in for itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The default rules</h2>
  <p>
    A rule is a set of lowercase substrings and a mode. A key matches when its
    name, lowercased, contains any of them. Matching on a substring is why
    <code>X-API-Key</code>, <code>authorization</code> and
    <code>customer_email</code> are all caught without every casing and prefix
    being listed.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Key names that match</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>mask</code></td>
        <td>
          <code>api_key</code>, <code>apikey</code>, <code>api-key</code>,
          <code>secret</code>, <code>password</code>, <code>passwd</code>,
          <code>access_token</code>, <code>refresh_token</code>,
          <code>id_token</code>, <code>bearer</code>,
          <code>authorization</code>, <code>auth_token</code>,
          <code>private_key</code>, <code>client_secret</code>,
          <code>session_token</code>, <code>credential</code>,
          <code>signature</code>, <code>x-api-key</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>partial</code></td>
        <td>
          <code>email</code>, <code>phone</code>, <code>ssn</code>,
          <code>credit_card</code>, <code>card_number</code>, <code>iban</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>hash</code></td>
        <td><code>user_id</code>, <code>userid</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    When a matched key holds an object or an array, everything underneath it is
    redacted too. <code>{'{"credential": {"value": "hunter2", "note": "prod"}}'}</code>
    loses both fields, not only the one whose name looked dangerous.
  </p>
  <p>
    The limit is worth stating plainly: rules match <strong>key names, not
    values</strong>. An API key a user pasted into the middle of a prompt,
    stored under a key called <code>content</code>, is not matched by anything
    here. Redaction stops the structured capture of credentials and identifiers.
    It is not a content scanner, and treating it as one will disappoint you.
  </p>

  <h2>The four modes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Mode</th><th>What happens to the value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>drop</code></td>
        <td>The key is removed entirely, as if it was never sent.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>mask</code></td>
        <td>The value is replaced with <code>[redacted]</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>partial</code></td>
        <td>
          The first two and last two characters survive, joined by an ellipsis:
          <code>alice@example.com</code> becomes <code>al…om</code>. Enough to
          recognise a value you already know, not enough to use. A value of six
          characters or fewer is masked entirely instead, because keeping four
          of five characters discloses most of it.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>hash</code></td>
        <td>
          The value is replaced with <code>sha256:</code> and the first twelve
          bytes of a keyed SHA-256 digest, as twenty-four hex characters.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    The built-in rules use three of the four. Nothing is dropped by default:
    <code>drop</code> is there for a rule you add, where even the presence of a
    field is more than you want to keep.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="what came back out"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -s "$MCPD/v1/api/traces/$TRACE" | jq .spans[0].input' },
      { out: '{' },
      { out: '  "question": "Where is my order A-1042?",' },
      { out: '  "api_key": "[redacted]",', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "email": "al…om",', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "user_id": "sha256:9f2c1b7d4a08e5c3f6b1d0a4"', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '}' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Why the hash is keyed</h2>
  <p>
    Hashing keeps a field useful. Equal inputs give equal digests under one key,
    so you can still group by a redacted user id, count how many distinct people
    hit a failure, and follow one of them across a session, without holding who
    they are.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unkeyed, that property is a hole. Identifiers come from small, guessable
    spaces, and anyone with the digest and this source can hash candidates until
    one matches. The key is what makes the digest unrecoverable rather than
    merely inconvenient.
  </p>
  <p>
    The built-in key is a constant in the source, so it is public. Set your own
    in any deployment that hashes anything.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    label="a deployment that hashes and expires"
    command={'mcpd --redact-key "$MCPD_REDACT_KEY" --retention-days 30'}
    secondary={[
      { label: 'as environment', command: 'MCPD_REDACT_KEY=… MCPD_RETENTION_DAYS=30 mcpd' },
      { label: 'redaction off', command: 'mcpd --no-redact' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Rotating the key breaks the link on purpose. Every digest changes, so rows
    written before the rotation can no longer be joined to rows written after
    it. That is what rotation is for, and it is also what it costs. Rotate when
    you want the link severed, not on a schedule out of habit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the second half of that cost before you rotate. Erasure by subject
    matches the stored digest, so every row written under the old key is beyond
    the reach of a deletion request from the moment the new key is in force.
    mcpd cannot tell you that this is what happened, because nothing records
    which key wrote which row. All it can do is refuse to let the resulting zero
    pass as an answer, and the section on a zero below is that refusal.
  </p>

  <h2>Every signal, not only spans</h2>
  <p>
    A secret leaks as readily through a log line as through a prompt, so the
    same redactor runs over all six signals on the way in:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Spans: <code>input</code>, <code>output</code>, <code>attributes</code> and <code>metadata</code>.</li>
    <li>Logs: the structured <code>fields</code>.</li>
    <li>Metrics: the <code>labels</code>.</li>
    <li>Scores: the <code>metadata</code>.</li>
    <li>Feedback: the <code>metadata</code>, and the free-text <code>comment</code>.</li>
    <li>Aspect notes: the sentence in <code>text</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A span's name and a log record's message are not passed through the
    redactor. They are identifiers, not payloads, and rewriting them would break
    grouping. Do not build a span name out of a customer's email address.
  </p>

  <h3>The run store is not redacted</h3>
  <p>
    Durable runs are the exception, and it is deliberate. The run store keeps a
    run's input, state, output and correlation, and every step's input, output
    and error, exactly as they were sent, with <code>user_id</code> unhashed.
    They are the workflow's working state: a later step reads them back, and a
    value the redactor had rewritten would change what the run does. The spans
    a run writes into the trace store take the ordinary ingest path, so the
    trace store holds them redacted and hashed like any other span. Retention
    and erasure both reach the run store, as the sections below say, and every
    read of a run through the API is recorded in the audit log.
  </p>

  <h2>Long strings and deep documents</h2>
  <p>
    Two bounds apply to everything, whether or not a rule matched.
  </p>
  <p>
    Any single string longer than 128 KiB is truncated, with
    <code>… [N bytes truncated]</code> appended so the reader knows the value is
    incomplete rather than short. The cut lands on a character boundary, never
    inside a multi-byte codepoint. One runaway prompt should not decide the size
    of your database.
  </p>
  <p>
    Walking stops at a depth of 32, and anything below that is replaced with
    <code>[too deeply nested]</code>. A pathological or self-referencing
    document arriving at an open ingest port cannot pin a CPU.
  </p>

  <h2>Retention</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--retention-days</code>, or <code>MCPD_RETENTION_DAYS</code>, deletes
    signals older than that many days. Omit it and nothing is ever deleted.
    Keeping data by default is the right way round: a tool that silently
    discarded last month's traces would be discovered at the worst moment.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pass runs on an hourly timer inside the daemon, not at query time. A
    retention sweep can delete a great many rows, and that work must never land
    inside somebody's request. Each pass removes, in one transaction, the spans,
    logs, metrics and trace rows whose <code>received_at</code> is older than
    the cutoff, and the scores, feedback and aspect notes whose own timestamp
    is, then logs how many rows went. Full-text index entries are cleared first,
    while the rows that name them still exist, so nothing is orphaned.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same pass, on the same window, trims the run store. It deletes every
    durable run whose root ended before the cutoff, with its child runs and
    everything stored for them, in batches so that workers are not held up
    while it works. A run that has not ended is never deleted, however long it
    has been waiting. Definitions are not trimmed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pass covers the project named by <code>--project</code> and every
    project in the <code>projects</code> block of the config file, not only the
    default. A project that sets its own <code>retention_days</code> there is
    swept on that figure, and the rest are swept on the daemon's. A project in
    neither place, one a sender named on its own, has no window and is never
    trimmed. That matters when one daemon serves several teams: a team that
    agreed to keep thirty days does not silently keep them because another
    team's setting was the only one the sweep read, and a team nobody wrote
    down keeps everything.
  </p>

  <h2>An erasure request about a person</h2>
  <p>
    <code>DELETE /v1/api/subjects/{'{user_id}'}</code> answers a request about
    one person in one call. Send the identifier the way your instrumentation set
    it. The daemon applies the same hashing it applied at ingest, so a
    deployment that hashes <code>user_id</code> does not need you to hash it
    first.
  </p>
  <p>
    The unit of erasure is the run, not the row. Only a root span usually
    carries a <code>user_id</code>, and the child spans are the ones holding the
    prompt and the completion, so deleting the rows that name the subject would
    leave the payload standing. A run is in scope when its trace projection
    names the subject or when any of its spans does, and then the whole run
    goes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Every span of that run, with the inputs and outputs it carried.</li>
    <li>The logs, metrics, scores, feedback and aspect notes hanging off it.</li>
    <li>
      Signals that name a <code>span_id</code> and no <code>trace_id</code>. A
      score or a log line pinned to one step of the subject's run is reached
      through the span ids of the runs in scope, not only through their trace
      ids.
    </li>
    <li>
      Log lines recorded against the person with no run to hang on, matched on
      their own <code>user_id</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Feedback the person wrote, matched on <code>author_id</code>, wherever it
      hangs. That row holds their own prose, and it can hang on somebody else's
      run, which is where it used to survive.
    </li>
    <li>
      Dataset cases captured from a run in scope. Those are emptied rather than
      deleted. The next section but one says why.
    </li>
    <li>
      On a daemon that serves durable runs, every run that has ended whose
      correlation carries the <code>user_id</code>, matched as you started the
      run with it because the run store keeps it unhashed, with its whole tree
      of child runs and everything the run store holds for them. A run of that
      person that has not ended is not touched: deleting it would corrupt the
      workflow it is part of. It is counted in <code>runs_in_flight</code> and
      named in <code>note</code>, so you can cancel it, or let it end, and send
      the request again.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The run store is erased first. A run's spans wait in its outbox until they
    are written into the trace store, so erasing the runs first deletes any
    span still waiting, and the trace store erasure then removes the ones
    already written.
  </p>

  <h3>What the reply carries</h3>
  <p>
    A count per signal, two counts for the run store, and six fields beside
    them carrying what a count cannot: what was deliberately left behind, and
    whether a small number can be read as "nothing about this person is stored
    here". <code>deleted</code> is the total of the six signal counts; the run
    counts and <code>aspect_notes_erased</code> are reported beside it, not
    added into it.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="one request, one person"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -sX DELETE "$MCPD/v1/api/subjects/u_8841?project=checkout" | jq' },
      { out: '{' },
      { out: '  "project": "checkout",' },
      { out: '  "traces": 1,', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "spans": 2,', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "logs": 1,', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "metrics": 0,', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "scores": 1,', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "feedback": 1,', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "deleted": 6,' },
      { out: '  "runs_erased": 1,', tone: 'note' },
      { out: '  "runs_in_flight": 0,' },
      { out: '  "dataset_items_erased": 1,', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '  "aspect_notes_erased": 0,' },
      { out: '  "datasets": [' },
      { out: '    "checkout-regressions"' },
      { out: '  ],' },
      { out: '  "deletes_pending": false,' },
      { out: '  "unmatchable_identifiers": 0,' },
      { out: '  "note": "1 captured dataset cases were emptied in place rather', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           than deleted, so a regression test is not destroyed', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           without anyone being told. They hold no payload any', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           more."', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '}' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>runs_erased</code></td>
          <td>
            Durable runs deleted from the run store, child runs included. Not
            counted in <code>deleted</code>. Absent when the daemon serves no
            runs
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>runs_in_flight</code></td>
          <td>
            The person's durable runs left whole because they have not ended.
            <code>note</code> names the runs to cancel. Absent when the daemon
            serves no runs
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>dataset_items_erased</code></td>
          <td>
            Captured cases emptied in place. They are not counted in
            <code>deleted</code>, because the rows are still there
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>aspect_notes_erased</code></td>
          <td>
            Aspect notes deleted. Not counted in <code>deleted</code>, which
            totals the six signal counts printed above it
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>datasets</code></td>
          <td>Which datasets those cases sit in, so you can go and look at them</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>deletes_pending</code></td>
          <td>
            True when the backend applies the deletes after answering, so the
            counts say what matched rather than what has already left the disk
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>unmatchable_identifiers</code></td>
          <td>
            Stored identifiers in this project that are in a form this request
            could not have matched
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>note</code></td>
          <td>
            Why the numbers above may not be the whole answer. Absent when they
            are
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Captured cases are emptied, not deleted</h3>
  <p>
    A dataset case captured from production holds a verbatim copy of the input,
    so erasing the run and leaving the copy would leave the person's words in
    the dataset. Deleting the case outright is a harm of a different kind:
    somebody captured that run to keep a bug from coming back, and destroying
    their regression test without a word is not a decision mcpd should make on
    its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the payload is overwritten and the row survives. The case reads
    <code>{'{"erased": true}'}</code> where its input and metadata were, its
    link back to the source run is cleared, and the reply names how many cases
    and which datasets. The case is visibly empty, which is something an
    operator can act on.
  </p>
  <p>
    One case is out of reach entirely, and tenancy is the reason. A case
    captured into a dataset that belongs to <em>another</em> project is not
    emptied and is not counted. The call is authorised for one project and touches one project, and
    an erasure that reached across that boundary would be a way to learn what is
    in a dataset you cannot read. Erase the person from that project too, with a
    key that reaches it.
  </p>

  <h3>What it does not do</h3>
  <p>
    It touches one project. <code>?project=</code> names it and the daemon's
    default project is used when you leave it out. A key that does not reach
    that project is refused with a 403 and nothing is deleted. Rows for the
    same person in a project the key cannot open are neither touched nor
    counted, so a count can never disclose that the subject exists somewhere
    else. Erasing a person from several projects is one call per project, with
    a key that reaches each.
  </p>
  <p>
    A subject with nothing stored is a 200 carrying zeroes, not a 404. The
    state you asked for holds, and a 404 would answer a question nobody is
    entitled to ask: whether that person exists anywhere this key can see. It is
    also why a repeat after a timeout is safe. The second call reports the
    smaller counts of a second pass rather than an error.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not a way to erase a person from your own systems. mcpd deletes what
    mcpd stored. Anything you forwarded to another OTLP endpoint has to be
    erased there too, and mcpd does not know what happened to it.
  </p>

  <h3>Keys and the record of it</h3>
  <p>
    Erasure needs an admin key when keys are configured, and falls back to the
    read keys when no admin key is set, so an existing deployment keeps the
    reach it had. It is written to the audit log with the action, the project
    and a digest of the key that asked, and a refused attempt is recorded the
    same way. Where <code>user_id</code> is hashed, the digest is what the row
    names: the audit log outlives the data it describes, so writing the raw
    identifier there would leave the name the person asked you to remove in a
    table retention does not sweep.
  </p>
  <p>
    One run at a time is still there.
    <code>DELETE /v1/api/traces/{'{trace_id}'}</code> removes a single run and
    everything attached to it, in one transaction, returning the number of rows
    deleted.
  </p>

  <h2>A zero from an erasure is the value to distrust</h2>
  <p>
    A zero is what an operator repeats to a person as "we hold nothing about
    you", and it is the one number this endpoint cannot prove. Stored
    identifiers are hashed with the redaction key, and nothing records which key
    hashed which row, so a zero can equally mean the key moved after those rows
    were written. mcpd cannot tell the two apart. What it can do is never hand
    back a bare zero that reads like the first one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here is the same subject, erased under a redaction key that has been rotated
    since the run was recorded. The counts are all zero and the row is still
    there:
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="after a rotation, the same request matches nothing"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -sX DELETE "$MCPD/v1/api/subjects/u_8841?project=checkout" | jq "{deleted, unmatchable_identifiers, note}"' },
      { out: '{' },
      { out: '  "deleted": 0,', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '  "unmatchable_identifiers": 0,' },
      { out: '  "note": "Nothing matched. Stored identifiers are hashed with', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           the redaction key, so this means no stored identifier', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           hashes to this subject under the key in force now. It', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           does not mean nothing about this person was ever', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           stored: rows written under a different redaction key', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           cannot be matched by this endpoint."', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '}' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    What the note can prove depends on the project. When nothing in it is
    attributed to anybody, there is no row a key change could be hiding, and the
    note says exactly that instead. Otherwise a zero is unproven: restore the
    redaction key those rows were written under and repeat the request.
  </p>
  <p>
    It reports rather than refuses, and that is a deliberate trade. Refusing
    would break a repeat after a timeout, it would make the reply for "never
    seen" differ from the reply for "erased an hour ago" and turn the endpoint
    into a way to ask whether a person is known to a project, and it would block
    every erasure in a deployment whose redaction setting ever changed,
    including the ones that correctly matched nothing.
  </p>

  <h3>Identifiers this request could not have matched</h3>
  <p>
    A rotation is invisible. A change to <em>whether</em> identifiers are hashed
    at all is not, and <code>unmatchable_identifiers</code> is what mcpd can see.
    A hashed identifier is recognisable by its <code>sha256:</code> prefix, so
    the store counts how many of the identifiers it holds are in the opposite
    form to the one this request carried: plaintext rows against a hashed
    request, or the reverse. Those rows were written under a different redaction
    setting, they may belong to the subject, and this request cannot address
    them.
  </p>

  <p>
    The count runs on every erasure, not only on a zero, and the reply below is
    why. This project was written to with redaction on, then written to again
    with it off. The request matched the newer rows and deleted them, and the
    two older identifiers are hashed, so this request could never have addressed
    them. A count that stopped at <code>deleted</code> would have read as a
    complete answer:
  </p>

  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="a partial erasure, which is the case worth catching"
    lines={[
      { cmd: 'curl -sX DELETE "$MCPD/v1/api/subjects/u_8841?project=checkout" | jq "{deleted, unmatchable_identifiers, note}"' },
      { out: '{' },
      { out: '  "deleted": 2,' },
      { out: '  "unmatchable_identifiers": 2,', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '  "note": "2 stored identifiers in this project are in a form', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           this request could not match, so rows written under a', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           different redaction setting were not searched and rows', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           about this person may remain. Restore the redaction', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           setting those rows were written under and repeat the', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '           request."', tone: 'warn' },
      { out: '}' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The count is over every identifier the project holds, not over the subject's
    own rows, so those two may belong to somebody else entirely. That is the
    most it can say. What it rules out is reading a successful-looking erasure
    as a complete one.
  </p>

  <h2>The guarantee is not the same on all three backends</h2>
  <p>
    SQLite and Postgres delete inside the transaction that answered you. When
    the reply arrives the rows are gone, and <code>deletes_pending</code> is
    <code>false</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    ClickHouse does not. Deletion there is an asynchronous mutation: the server
    accepts the statement and applies it in the background, so the reply says
    what matched rather than what has already left the disk. Those erasures come
    back with <code>deletes_pending</code> set to <code>true</code>, which is
    mcpd telling you the weaker of the two guarantees is the one in force. The
    dataset cases are the exception on that backend. They are rewritten under
    their own key rather than mutated, so the emptied case is what any read
    returns from the moment the reply is sent. A mutation would have left the
    old text readable until it finished.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have to certify to somebody that the data is gone by a given moment,
    that is the difference to write down. On ClickHouse, confirm the mutation
    completed on the server before you answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Before the port is reachable</h2>
  <p>
    Redaction protects the values inside a trace. It does nothing about who can
    read the trace. mcpd warns at startup when it binds a non-loopback address
    with open ingest or an open read API, because stored prompts and completions
    are then readable by anyone who can reach the port. Set
    <code>--ingest-key</code> and <code>--read-key</code> first, and read
    <a href="/deploy" class="link">Docker and Kubernetes</a>.
  </p>

